/*
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload

# ===========================================================================
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING ANY RULE
# ===========================================================================
# 1. Cloudflare Pages applies EVERY matching rule, not the most specific one.
#    When two matching rules set the same header name the values are JOINED
#    WITH A COMMA — "If a header is applied twice in the _headers file, the
#    values are joined with a comma separator."
#    (developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/headers/)
#    So two overlapping Cache-Control rules do not fall back to the narrower
#    one, they produce "public, max-age=2592000, public, max-age=3600". Every
#    Cache-Control rule below is keyed on a distinct file extension so that no
#    two of them can ever match the same path. Keep it that way.
#
# 2. Pages redirects every /foo.html to /foo before serving anything — "Pages
#    will also redirect HTML pages to their extension-less counterparts."
#    (developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/serving-pages/)
#    A rule written against an .html path therefore lands on a 308 and never
#    on the response that actually carries the page. Any rule for an HTML page
#    must be written for the extensionless form. Both forms are listed here;
#    the .html one costs nothing and covers direct hits on the redirect.
#    This does NOT apply to .pdf — only HTML is extension-stripped, confirmed
#    live: /assets/glp1-ch1-sample-7k2m9x.pdf returns 200 directly with its
#    X-Robots-Tag intact.
#
# 3. Only one splat (*) is allowed per URL pattern, and a splat matches
#    greedily across "/" — so /assets/*.png also covers
#    /assets/credentials/whatever.png. That is why there is no separate rule
#    for the credentials folder.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser caching
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing below is marked `immutable`, and that is deliberate. Our filenames
# are not content-hashed: assets/styles.css and assets/good-years-cover.jpg
# keep those names forever, and optimize-images.py regenerates the -600.avif /
# -600.webp derivatives under the same names whenever a source image changes.
# `immutable` tells the browser never to revalidate before max-age expires, so
# replacing an image in place would strand returning visitors on the old bytes
# with no way to force a refresh short of renaming the file. A long max-age
# already buys nearly all of the performance; `immutable` only adds the part
# we cannot undo.
#
# HTML is deliberately absent from this section. Pages already serves HTML as
# "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate" (verified live on
# book.laveenaarchers.com), which is exactly what we want — a content edit is
# visible on the next request. The only pattern that would catch our
# extensionless HTML URLs is /*, and per note 1 that would also match every
# asset and comma-collide with the rules below. Leave HTML on the default.
#
# PDFs are absent for the same reason as HTML: the Pages default (4 hours,
# must-revalidate) is already right for a lead magnet that may be revised in
# place, and adding a /assets/*.pdf block risks colliding with the gated-PDF
# rules further down if either ever grows a shared header name.

# Images: book covers, the portrait, the certificate scans, and the .avif /
# .webp derivatives of all of them. In practice these never change — a new
# image gets a new filename. 30 days covers essentially every repeat visit,
# and is short enough that an in-place replacement heals on its own within a
# month rather than never. .jpg and .jpeg need separate rules because a
# pattern may hold only one splat and *.jpg does not match *.jpeg.
/assets/*.avif
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000, must-revalidate

/assets/*.webp
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000, must-revalidate

/assets/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000, must-revalidate

/assets/*.jpg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000, must-revalidate

/assets/*.jpeg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000, must-revalidate

# Hand-edited source assets. assets/styles.css, assets/site.js,
# assets/analytics.js, assets/glp1-quiz.js and assets/hope-press-colophon.svg
# all get edited in place under the same filename, so they need to go stale
# quickly. One hour, down from the 4-hour Pages default, so a style or script
# fix reaches returning visitors the same morning it ships. must-revalidate
# means an edge that is holding an expired copy has to check rather than serve
# it. This is the one place where a shorter TTL is the correct trade.
/assets/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

/assets/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, must-revalidate

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gated lead magnets
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Each is delivered by email or from a noindex thanks page, so none of them
# should be findable in search. Without these rules a reader can download the
# magnet without ever joining the list. Verified live: .pdf is not
# extension-stripped, so these single rules are sufficient as written.
/assets/glp1-ch1-sample-7k2m9x.pdf
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, noarchive, nofollow

/assets/companion-workbook.pdf
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, noarchive, nofollow

/assets/glp1-support-kit.pdf
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, noarchive, nofollow

/assets/glp1-resistant-starch-trick.pdf
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, noarchive, nofollow

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gated page: the Nature GLP-1 piece
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the one that was actually broken. The .html rule alone put the
# noindex on a 308 while /blog/nature-glp1 served the full article with no
# X-Robots-Tag at all — verified live before this change. The page also has no
# <meta name="robots"> of its own, so the header was the only thing standing
# between a gated article and Google, and it was pointed at the wrong URL.
/blog/nature-glp1
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, noarchive, nofollow

/blog/nature-glp1.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, noarchive, nofollow

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Thanks pages and other non-public pages
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These already carry <meta name="robots" content="noindex,follow"> in the
# page source, so unlike /blog/nature-glp1 they were not exposed. The headers
# below are belt and braces: they reach crawlers and fetchers that never parse
# the HTML, and they hold even if a future template edit drops the meta tag.
#
# The value is "noindex, follow", NOT the "noindex, noarchive, nofollow" used
# for the PDFs above. That is on purpose and should not be tidied into
# matching. Each of these pages deliberately says `follow` in its own meta
# tag, and Google combines robots directives by taking the most restrictive —
# so adding `nofollow` in a header would silently override the page author's
# choice and stop link equity flowing from the thanks pages to the book pages
# they link to. Mirror the meta tag; do not extend it.
/glp1-chapter-thanks
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/glp1-chapter-thanks.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/support-kit-thanks
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/support-kit-thanks.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/resistant-starch-thanks
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/resistant-starch-thanks.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/review
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/review.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/training
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/training.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/services
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/services.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

# 404.html is reachable as an ordinary asset — /404 returns HTTP 200, not 404,
# so the "it is a 404, crawlers ignore it" assumption does not hold here. Its
# meta tag says plain `noindex` with no `follow`, so this mirrors that exactly.
/404
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/404.html
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
